<!--
  Zimbra authentication rules (audit.log)          range 100831-100859,
                                                   100811-100813, 100835, 100907-100919,
                                                   100990, 100991, 100998, 100999
  Decoder zimbra-soap-authfail (mailbox.log) for 100990/100991
  Decoders: zimbra-audit and zimbra-audit-fail (decoders/0001-zimbra_decoders.xml)

  3.1 (2026-10-11): one policy for every successful login, with no site
  data needed. With the shipped lists left as they are:
    ordinary account, private address ........ level 2 (recorded, no alert)
      first time this (account, address) pair is seen: level 3 (100838)
    ordinary account, public address .......... level 2, first time for the
                                                pair level 5 (100854/100837)
    administrator-class login, private ........ level 3 (100847/100848)
    administrator-class login, public ......... level 8 (100849)
    success after repeated failures ........... level 14 same address (100846),
                                                level 11 same account (100833)
  "Administrator-class" uses only what the product itself defines: the
  admin SOAP endpoint over http(s) (the admin console, port 7071 by
  default), cmd=AdminAuth, and the built-in account names admin@,
  administrator@ and root@. Zimbra's own tools (user agent zm* or ZCS) are
  not logins anyone made and stay at level 0.
  "Private" is 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16, 127/8, 100.64/10, fc00::/7 and
  fe80::/10, written into the rules. ::1 is left out on purpose: a negated
  ::1 in 4.14.7 also excludes every IPv4 address. etc/lists/zimbra-internal-
  networks is optional tuning: an address listed there is never treated as
  external (an office NAT address, a proxy with a public address).

  Public addresses are level 5 only the first time for each pair, not on
  every login: Z-Push logs in to Zimbra on every request it relays, 435
  times a day from public (mobile) addresses on the two measured servers,
  against 2.4 new (account, address) pairs a day.

  3.0 (2026-10-11), measured against 8.8 days of real audit lines from two
  servers (61,000 lines) and 41 days of their alerts:

  * 100845, 100847 and their children had never fired. <action> compares as
    a plain string by default, so "^Auth$|^AdminAuth$" was looked for
    literally. Fixed with type="pcre2".
  * Proxied lines (Z-Push, a reverse proxy: "oip=client, proxy") decoded with
    no srcip and no user, so brute force through a proxy was invisible.
    The decoder now takes the client address (see the decoder file).
  * 100845 also matched failures (a failed login is "cmd=Auth ... error=").
  * 100846 hung off every authentication_success rule in the ruleset; it is
    now limited to Zimbra events.
  * Only the copy read from /opt/zimbra/log/audit.log counts. With
    zimbraLogToSyslog set the same lines also arrive through syslog
    ("zimbra_audit"), and counting both halved every threshold.

  3.2 (2026-10-11):
  * Failures have their own decoder (zimbra-audit-fail) and base rule
    (100835). Its fts key marks the first failure of an (account, address)
    pair (100811); spraying (100843) and distributed guessing (100844) count
    those, so "eight accounts" and "ten addresses" are distinct values.
  * The unknown-account line (zimbra_additional_logging) is a failure.
  * The lockout record (error=account lockout due to too many failed logins)
    is a child of 100841 and always level 10.
  * SSOAuth logins follow the login policy; a recovery-code login is level 5
    (100812); a lockout lifted by a login is level 3 (100813).
  * Administrative changes (cmd=ModifyAccount ... in audit.log): 100908-100919.

  3.3 (2026-10-15), account security:
  * The decoders read every field from its place in the line (see
    0001-zimbra_decoders.xml): a URL or a user agent can no longer set the
    source address, the account or "this login failed".
  * A Zimbra tool user agent (zmprov, ZCS...) silences a login only for
    Zimbra's own "zimbra" account from no address or a private one
    (100853); the other rules no longer skip those user agents, so a
    stolen password used with "User-Agent: zmprov" is reported as usual.
  * The rules below do not look for "error=", "preauth" or "admin=true"
    anywhere in the line any more, only in the message itself.
  * An SMTP AUTH failure is recorded at level 2 (100999): Postfix's own
    line raises the built-in 3332 for the same attempt.
  * A login from an address that Postfix reported for repeated SMTP AUTH
    failures (built-in 3357) is level 14 (100998).
  * Cross-pack login groups: every rule a login event can end at carries
    jt_auth_success, every rule a failed attempt can end at jt_auth_failure,
    and the lockout record jt_auth_lockout (one per attempt: the syslog
    copies and Zimbra's own tools end at level 0 and carry none).
    DelegateAuth (an administrator opening another mailbox) is not tagged:
    it is not a login of the account it names.

  Counted correlation uses pack groups (if_matched_group), not
  if_matched_sid: an event is only counted for the rule it finally matched.
    zimbra_login_failed   every failed attempt (100841, 100857, 100811, 100999, 100991)
    zimbra_newpair_fail   first failure of an (account, address) pair (100811)
    zimbra_auth_failures  the brute-force alerts (100842, 100843, 100844, 100836, 100859)
    zimbra_auth_success   every real login (not Zimbra's own tools)
    zimbra_external_login logins from a public address
-->
<group name="zimbra,authentication,">

  <rule id="100840" level="0">
    <decoded_as>zimbra-audit</decoded_as>
    <location>/opt/zimbra/log/audit.log</location>
    <description>Zimbra authentication log (base rule)</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- The syslog copy (zimbraLogToSyslog, program "zimbra_audit"): decoded,
       then dropped here, before the generic syslog rule 2501 turns every
       copied failure into a second level 5 alert. -->
  <rule id="100839" level="0">
    <decoded_as>zimbra-audit</decoded_as>
    <location type="pcre2">^(?!.*/opt/zimbra/log/audit\.log$)</location>
    <description>Zimbra audit line received through syslog, a copy of audit.log (not counted)</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- ── failures ── -->

  <!-- 3.2: failures have their own decoder, zimbra-audit-fail (see the
       decoder file), so they no longer pass through 100840. The decoder
       takes every cmd= line with error= and the unknown-account sentence
       Zimbra writes when zimbra_additional_logging is on. -->
  <rule id="100835" level="0">
    <decoded_as>zimbra-audit-fail</decoded_as>
    <location>/opt/zimbra/log/audit.log</location>
    <description>Zimbra authentication failure log (base rule)</description>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100907" level="0">
    <decoded_as>zimbra-audit-fail</decoded_as>
    <location type="pcre2">^(?!.*/opt/zimbra/log/audit\.log$)</location>
    <description>Zimbra audit failure line received through syslog, a copy of audit.log (not counted)</description>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100841" level="5">
    <if_sid>100835</if_sid>
    <description>Zimbra authentication failed: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip) ($(zimbra.error))</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failed,zimbra_login_failed,jt_auth_failure,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3.2: Zimbra's own record of locking an account is
         cmd=Auth; account=x; error=account lockout due to too many failed logins;
       (LdapLockoutPolicy.java:222, 10.1.20). It contains "error=" and
       "lockout", so in 3.1 it was claimed by 100841 or 100857 and the
       level 10 lockout alert (then a sibling of 100841) could come out at
       level 5 or 6. It is now a child of 100841, and 100857 excludes it.
       Not in zimbra_login_failed: Zimbra writes it next to the failure line
       of the same attempt, which is already counted. -->
  <rule id="100856" level="10">
    <if_sid>100841</if_sid>
    <field name="zimbra.error" type="pcre2">lockout due to too many failed logins</field>
    <description>[WARN] Zimbra locked an account after repeated failed logins: $(dstuser) (last attempt from: $(srcip))</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,jt_auth_lockout,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- A login attempt on an account that is already locked
       ("authentication failed for [x], account lockout"). -->
  <rule id="100857" level="6">
    <if_sid>100841</if_sid>
    <field name="zimbra.error" type="pcre2">^(?!.*lockout due to too many failed logins)(?i:.*(?:lockout|locked))</field>
    <description>Login attempt on a locked Zimbra account: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failed,zimbra_login_failed,jt_auth_failure,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3.2: the first failure of an (account, address) pair (fts of
       zimbra-audit-fail). Counted twice: as a failure (zimbra_login_failed,
       for the volume rule 100842) and as a new pair (zimbra_newpair_fail,
       for 100843 and 100844, which need distinct accounts or addresses). -->
  <rule id="100811" level="5">
    <if_sid>100841</if_sid>
    <if_fts />
    <description>Zimbra authentication failed, first failure for this account from this address: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip) ($(zimbra.error))</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failed,zimbra_login_failed,zimbra_newpair_fail,jt_auth_failure,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3.3: an SMTP AUTH failure. Postfix authenticates through saslauthd,
       which asks Zimbra (the admin SOAP port, oproto=smtp in the context),
       so one wrong SMTP password is written twice: here, with the account,
       and in zimbra.log by Postfix
         postfix/submission/smtpd[123]: warning: unknown[203.0.113.5]: SASL LOGIN authentication failed: UGFzc3dvcmQ6
       which the built-in rule 3332 already raises at level 5 (and 3357
       for eight in two minutes from one address). This copy is recorded
       at level 2 and still counted (100842-100844, jt_auth_failure). The
       first failure of each (account, address) pair stays at 100811
       (level 5), because that is the only alert naming the account. -->
  <rule id="100999" level="2">
    <if_sid>100841</if_sid>
    <field name="zimbra.oproto" type="pcre2">^smtp$</field>
    <description>Zimbra SMTP AUTH failure (Postfix reports the attempt as well): $(dstuser) from: $(srcip) ($(zimbra.error))</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failed,zimbra_login_failed,jt_auth_failure,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3.3: SOAP requests refused for their credentials (mailbox.log)
       Decoder: zimbra-soap-authfail (0002-zimbra_mailbox_decoders.xml)
       Every refused SOAP login writes this line, and until 3.3 nothing
       decoded it: the built-in 2501 raised each one at level 5, next to
       100841 for the same attempt from audit.log (tested). The base rule
       records them at level 0; audit.log stays the counted copy.
       The exception is the second factor, which audit.log never sees: after
       a correct password the client is asked for a code, and a wrong code
       ends here only. Two-factor is a Network Edition feature (and the
       open-source zimbra-maldua-2fa extension); without it nothing fires.
       Defined here, before 100842: a rule counted through if_matched_group
       has to be loaded before the counting rule (tested: from
       08-zimbra_soap_rules.xml the twelfth wrong code stayed at 100991). -->
  <rule id="100990" level="0">
    <decoded_as>zimbra-soap-authfail</decoded_as>
    <description>Zimbra SOAP request refused for its credentials (counted from audit.log)</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- Each wrong code is one failed login: jt_auth_failure, and counted
       with the password failures (zimbra_login_failed), so a run of codes
       guessed from one address raises 100842 (twelve in five minutes).
       The password step before it was logged as a success (100845), which
       is why one wrong code already says the password is known. Level 5,
       not more: people mistype codes. -->
  <rule id="100991" level="5">
    <if_sid>100990</if_sid>
    <location>/opt/zimbra/log/mailbox.log</location>
    <field name="zimbra.error" type="pcre2">^(?:invalid two-factor code|invalid TOTP code)$</field>
    <description>Zimbra rejected a two-factor code after a correct password: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>zimbra_login_failed,jt_auth_failure,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Brute force from one address. Through Z-Push or a reverse proxy the
       address is the client the proxy reported (first oip entry). -->
  <rule id="100842" level="10" frequency="12" timeframe="300">
    <if_matched_group>zimbra_login_failed</if_matched_group>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>[WARN] Repeated Zimbra authentication failures, possible brute force: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,zimbra_auth_failures,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- The same, from a public address (and not listed in the optional
       zimbra-internal-networks): the rule an active response may use. -->
  <rule id="100859" level="10">
    <if_sid>100842</if_sid>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="not_address_match_key">etc/lists/zimbra-internal-networks</list>
    <description>[WARN] Repeated Zimbra authentication failures from an external address, possible brute force: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,zimbra_auth_failures,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Password spraying: one address, many accounts, few tries each.
       3.2: counts first-seen (account, address) failure pairs (100811)
       from one address, so eight means eight different accounts. 3.1 used
       different_user, which counted seven failures of one account and one
       of another as eight. A pair counts once, ever: an address that sprays
       the same list again later is still caught by the volume rule 100842. -->
  <rule id="100843" level="12" frequency="8" timeframe="600">
    <if_matched_group>zimbra_newpair_fail</if_matched_group>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>[HIGH] One source failed to log in to eight or more different Zimbra accounts, possible password spraying: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,zimbra_auth_failures,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100836" level="12">
    <if_sid>100843</if_sid>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="not_address_match_key">etc/lists/zimbra-internal-networks</list>
    <description>[HIGH] One external source tried many different accounts, possible password spraying: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,zimbra_auth_failures,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- One account failing from many addresses: guessing spread over a
       botnet, which no per-address rule (and no firewall block) sees.
       3.2: counts first-seen failure pairs of one account (ten different
       addresses), not different_srcip (see 100843). -->
  <rule id="100844" level="10" frequency="10" timeframe="600">
    <if_matched_group>zimbra_newpair_fail</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>zimbra.account</same_field>
    <description>[WARN] One Zimbra account failed to log in from ten or more different addresses, distributed password guessing: $(dstuser)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_failures,zimbra_auth_failures,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ── successes ── -->

  <!-- Every successful login, recorded at level 2 (about 4,800 a day on the
       two measured servers). The children below raise the ones the policy
       in the header asks for. 3.2: SSOAuth (a SAML, JWT or SPNEGO login,
       LdapProvisioning.ssoAuthAccount) is a login too; before 3.2 it was
       never counted. The "info=" line Zimbra writes when a login lifts a
       lockout is 100813, not a second login. -->
  <rule id="100845" level="2">
    <if_sid>100840</if_sid>
    <action type="pcre2">^(?:Auth|AdminAuth|SSOAuth)$</action>
    <match type="pcre2">^[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=\w[\w ]*;(?!.*? info=)</match>
    <description>Zimbra authentication succeeded: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <group>authentication_success,zimbra_auth_success,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Zimbra's own tools (zmprov, zmmailbox, zmconfigd, internal "ZCS")
       authenticate as the zimbra admin about once a minute. 3.1: also
       matches the bare "ZCS" user agent, which has no version.
       3.3: the user agent is whatever the client sends, so it is not
       enough on its own: until 3.2 any login with "ZCS" or "zmprov/1" as
       its user agent was dropped here, a stolen password included. Now
       the tool must also log in as Zimbra's built-in "zimbra" account and
       from no address (localhost, which Zimbra does not log) or a private
       one. Measured on two servers in ten days: every zm*/ZCS login was
       account=zimbra, from no address or the server's own. Level 0 is
       tried before the siblings below, so they no longer need their own
       user-agent exclusion. -->
  <rule id="100853" level="0">
    <if_sid>100845</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">^[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:(?:o?ip=(?:::ffff:)?(?:10\.|127\.|192\.168\.|172\.(?:1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.|100\.(?:6[4-9]|[7-9]\d|1[01]\d|12[0-7])\.)|(?!o?ip=)\w+=)(?:[^;\[\]]|;;)*;)*?ua=(?:zm[a-z]+|ZCS)[/;].*\] security - cmd=\w+; account=zimbra;</match>
    <description>Zimbra command-line tool authenticated (routine)</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3.2: a login with a password-reset recovery code (the "forgot
       password" code mailed to the account's recovery address):
         cmd=Auth; account=x; mode=recoveryCode;   (or "recoveryCode - account unlocked")
       (LdapProvisioning.recoveryCodeBasedAuthAccount, 10.1.20). Whoever
       controls the recovery address controls the account, so it is
       visible, from any address. Still a login for 100846/100833. -->
  <rule id="100812" level="5">
    <if_sid>100845</if_sid>
    <field name="zimbra.mode" type="pcre2">^recoveryCode</field>
    <description>Zimbra login with a password-reset recovery code: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip) ($(zimbra.mode))</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_success,zimbra_auth_success,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3.2: a successful login after the lockout period ended lifts the
       lockout and Zimbra writes an extra line before the login line:
         cmd=Auth; account=x; info=account re-activated from lockout status upon successful login;
       (LdapLockoutPolicy.successfulLogin, 10.1.20). The login itself is
       the next line (100845), so this one is not counted as a login. -->
  <rule id="100813" level="3">
    <if_sid>100840</if_sid>
    <action>Auth</action>
    <field name="zimbra.info" type="pcre2">re-activated from lockout</field>
    <description>Zimbra account unlocked by a successful login after its lockout expired: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- Administrator-class login (see the header). 3.1: was level 8 for the
       admin console only; now level 3, with the address deciding the rest.
       saslauthd's SMTP AUTH checks use the admin port with an smtp:// URL
       and are ordinary logins. -->
  <rule id="100847" level="3">
    <if_sid>100845</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">^(?:[^\[]*\[[^\]\s]*:https?://[^\]\s]*/service/admin/soap|[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=AdminAuth;|[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=\w+;.*? account=(?:admin|administrator|root)@)</match>
    <description>Zimbra administrator-class login: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_success,zimbra_auth_success,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100848" level="3">
    <if_sid>100847</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip>172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip>192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip>127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip>100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip>fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip>fe80::/10</srcip>
    <description>Zimbra administrator-class login from a private address: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_success,zimbra_auth_success,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3.1: was level 14. -->
  <rule id="100849" level="8">
    <if_sid>100847</if_sid>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="not_address_match_key">etc/lists/zimbra-internal-networks</list>
    <description>Zimbra administrator-class login from a public address: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_success,zimbra_auth_success,zimbra_external_login,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Ordinary account from a public address: recorded (level 2) for 100852,
       raised the first time for the pair (100837). -->
  <rule id="100854" level="2">
    <if_sid>100845</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">^(?![^\[]*\[[^\]\s]*:https?://[^\]\s]*/service/admin/soap|[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=AdminAuth;|[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=\w+;.*? account=(?:admin|administrator|root)@)</match>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="not_address_match_key">etc/lists/zimbra-internal-networks</list>
    <description>Zimbra login from a public address: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <group>authentication_success,zimbra_auth_success,zimbra_external_login,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100837" level="5">
    <if_sid>100854</if_sid>
    <if_fts />
    <description>First Zimbra login of this account from this public address: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_success,zimbra_auth_success,zimbra_external_login,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Ordinary account from a private address, first time for the pair.
       Later logins of the pair stay at 100845 (level 2): a phone or a
       monitoring probe is reported once per address, ever. -->
  <rule id="100838" level="3">
    <if_sid>100845</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">^(?![^\[]*\[[^\]\s]*:https?://[^\]\s]*/service/admin/soap|[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=AdminAuth;|[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=\w+;.*? account=(?:admin|administrator|root)@)</match>
    <srcip>10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip>172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip>192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip>127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip>100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip>fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip>fe80::/10</srcip>
    <if_fts />
    <description>First Zimbra login of this account from this private address: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <group>authentication_success,zimbra_auth_success,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Cracked: failures from an address, then a success from it. Attached
       through if_group to every Zimbra login rule above. Level 14 stays: it
       has to be above the level 12 of the spraying alerts (100843/100836)
       it correlates with, or 4.14.7 skips them (see 100833; tested). -->
  <rule id="100846" level="14" timeframe="900">
    <if_group>zimbra_auth_success</if_group>
    <if_matched_group>zimbra_auth_failures</if_matched_group>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>[HIGH] Zimbra: many failures then a success from the same source, the credentials appear to have been cracked: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>


  <!-- One account logging in from three addresses it has never used before,
       all public, within an hour. 3.1: counts the first-seen pairs of 100837.
       It used different_srcip before, which in 4.14.7 counts earlier events
       whose address differs from the CURRENT one, not distinct addresses:
       two logins from one address and one from a second already made
       "three sources", so every phone changing its mobile address within an
       hour of use would have raised a level 13 (tested). Measured: at most
       two new public pairs per account per hour in 8.8 days. -->
  <rule id="100852" level="13" frequency="3" timeframe="3600">
    <if_matched_sid>100837</if_matched_sid>
    <same_user />
    <description>[HIGH] One account logged in from three public addresses it had never used before within an hour, the credentials may have leaked: $(dstuser)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    </rule>

  <!-- preauth: a login made with the domain's preauth key, no password.
       An SSO integration does this for every login, so 3.1 records each
       one (level 2, was 13 per login) and raises the fact that preauth is
       in use at most once a day (100831); on a server without an SSO
       integration the first one is the news. Administrator accounts through
       preauth are raised every time (100832). -->
  <rule id="100850" level="2">
    <if_sid>100840</if_sid>
    <action>PreAuth</action>
    <description>Zimbra preauth login: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1550.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>authentication_success,jt_auth_success,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100832" level="12">
    <if_sid>100850</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">^[^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=\w+;.*? (?:admin=true;|account=(?:admin|administrator|root)@)</match>
    <description>[HIGH] preauth login to a Zimbra administrator account; unless an SSO integration does this, the preauth key may have leaked: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1550.001</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100831" level="10" ignore="86400">
    <if_sid>100850</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">^(?![^\[]*\[\S*\] \[(?:\w+=(?:[^;]|;;)*;)*\] security - cmd=\w+;.*? (?:admin=true;|account=(?:admin|administrator|root)@))</match>
    <description>[WARN] preauth logins are in use on this Zimbra server (reported once a day); unless an SSO integration uses the domain preauth key, it may have leaked: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1550.001</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- DelegateAuth: an administrator takes a session as another account
       (admin console "View Mail", or a tool holding an admin token).
       3.1: level 5 to 3 from a private address (administrator-class); the
       first time for an (administrator, account, address) triple level 5
       (100896); from a public address level 8 (100858, was 12). Zimbra's
       own tools are excluded. -->
  <rule id="100855" level="3">
    <if_sid>100840</if_sid>
    <action>DelegateAuth</action>
    <match type="pcre2">^(?![^\[]*\[\S*\] \[name=zimbra;(?:(?:o?ip=(?:::ffff:)?(?:10\.|127\.|192\.168\.|172\.(?:1[6-9]|2\d|3[01])\.|100\.(?:6[4-9]|[7-9]\d|1[01]\d|12[0-7])\.)|(?!o?ip=)\w+=)(?:[^;\[\]]|;;)*;)*?ua=(?:zm[a-z]+|ZCS)[/;])</match>
    <description>A Zimbra administrator opened a session as another account: $(srcuser) as $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1550.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100896" level="5">
    <if_sid>100855</if_sid>
    <srcip>10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip>172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip>192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip>127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip>100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip>fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip>fe80::/10</srcip>
    <if_fts />
    <description>A Zimbra administrator opened a session as another account for the first time from this address: $(srcuser) as $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1550.001</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100858" level="8">
    <if_sid>100855</if_sid>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="not_address_match_key">etc/lists/zimbra-internal-networks</list>
    <description>A Zimbra administrator session from a public address took over another account: $(srcuser) as $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1550.001</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- An account that was under password guessing (one address or many,
       100842/100859/100844) logged in, from anywhere. The real owner
       logging in looks the same, hence level 11, not 12. A correlation rule
       only uses an alert of a LOWER level than its own (4.14.7 skips events
       already matched at its level or above), so this one has to be above
       the level 10 of the brute-force alerts; at 10 it never fired (tested).
       same_field on a dynamic copy of the account, because same_user does
       not match here. -->
  <rule id="100833" level="11" timeframe="900">
    <if_group>zimbra_auth_success</if_group>
    <if_matched_group>zimbra_auth_failures</if_matched_group>
    <same_field>zimbra.account</same_field>
    <description>[WARN] Zimbra account logged in after a run of failed logins for it; check that it was the owner: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- 3.3: a login from an address that Postfix had just reported for
       repeated SMTP AUTH failures (built-in 3357: eight in two minutes from
       one address). Those failures reach audit.log only for accounts that
       exist, so a spray of guessed names over SMTP can end in a correct
       guess without any Zimbra failure rule having counted it (100846
       needs one). Postfix sees every attempt. The success is the audit.log
       line of any protocol (an SMTP AUTH through saslauthd carries the
       client as oip=). Level 14, above the level 10 of 3357, which 4.14.7
       requires of a correlation rule. -->
  <rule id="100998" level="14" timeframe="900">
    <if_group>zimbra_auth_success</if_group>
    <if_matched_sid>3357</if_matched_sid>
    <same_source_ip />
    <description>[HIGH] Zimbra login from an address that had just failed SMTP AUTH repeatedly, the password appears to have been guessed: $(dstuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1110.003</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>credential_access,</group>
  </rule>

</group>

<!-- ==========================================================
     3.2: administrative changes (audit.log)          100908-100919

     Every admin SOAP handler that changes the directory logs to audit.log
     with the attributes it changed (service/admin/*.java, 10.1.20):
       cmd=ModifyAccount; name=user1@example.com; zimbraIsAdminAccount=TRUE;
     In 3.1 all of them ended at 100840 (level 0). They arrive through the
     admin console, zmprov (without -l) and any tool holding an admin
     token; zmprov -l writes to LDAP directly and logs nothing here.
     Measured: none in 9 days on two servers, so every one is visible.

       any administrative change ............................ 3  (100908)
       account and domain lifecycle, configuration .......... 5  (100918)
       a credential-bearing attribute (line withheld) ....... 5  (100917)
       zimlets, delegated-admin constraints ................. 8  (100916)
       mail forwarding set by an administrator .............. 10 (100914)
       password reset of an administrator-class account ..... 10 (100915)
       domain preauth key set (line withheld) ............... 10 (100913)
       administrator rights granted ......................... 12 (100910)
       MTA trusted networks opened to every address ......... 12 (100911)
       any of these from a public address ................... 12 (100912/100919)
     user is the account changed, srcuser the administrator (the session's
     name=), srcip the administrator's client.
     Not logged by Zimbra at all, so not covered: GrantRight/RevokeRight
     (delegated rights), changes a user makes to their own preferences
     (see 100815 in 08-zimbra_soap_rules.xml), zmprov -l.
     ========================================================== -->
<group name="zimbra,account_changed,">

  <rule id="100908" level="3">
    <if_sid>100840</if_sid>
    <action type="pcre2">^(?!(?:Auth|AdminAuth|SSOAuth|PreAuth|DelegateAuth|DistributionListAction|SubscribeDistributionList|CheckPasswordStrength)$)</action>
    <description>Zimbra administrative change: $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- A distribution-list owner managing the list, a user subscribing,
       an administrator checking a password's strength: recorded only. -->
  <rule id="100909" level="2">
    <if_sid>100840</if_sid>
    <action type="pcre2">^(?:DistributionListAction|SubscribeDistributionList|CheckPasswordStrength)$</action>
    <description>Zimbra list or password-strength operation: $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser)</description>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100910" level="12">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <action type="pcre2">^(?:CreateAccount|ModifyAccount|CreateCalendarResource|ModifyCalendarResource)$</action>
    <match type="pcre2">(?i)\bzimbraIs(?:Delegated|Domain)?AdminAccount=TRUE;</match>
    <description>[HIGH] Zimbra administrator rights granted: $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- zimbraMtaMyNetworks is Postfix mynetworks: every address listed may
       relay without logging in. -->
  <rule id="100911" level="12">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">\bzimbraMtaMyNetworks=(?:[^;]*[\s,])?(?:0\.0\.0\.0/0|::/0|0/0)[\s,;]</match>
    <description>[HIGH] Zimbra MTA trusted networks set to every address, the server is an open relay: $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <group>spam,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Lines carrying a credential end at 100913/100917 (and 100919), which
       withhold the log line; this one shows it. -->
  <rule id="100912" level="12">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">^(?!.*\bzimbra\w*(?:BindPassword|AuthPassword|CertificatePassword|SourcePassword|SpecificPassword|UCPassword|Secret|TokenKey|PreAuthKey)=[^;\s])</match>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="not_address_match_key">etc/lists/zimbra-internal-networks</list>
    <description>[HIGH] Zimbra administrative change made from a public address: $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100913" level="10">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">\bzimbraPreAuthKey=[^;\s]</match>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <description>[WARN] Zimbra domain preauth key set; whoever holds it can log in as any account of the domain (log line withheld, it contains the key): $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1556</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100914" level="10">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <action type="pcre2">^(?:CreateAccount|ModifyAccount)$</action>
    <match type="pcre2">(?i)\bzimbra(?:Pref)?MailForwardingAddress=[^;\s]|\bzimbraPrefMailLocalDeliveryDisabled=TRUE;</match>
    <description>[WARN] Zimbra mail forwarding set on an account by an administrator: $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1114.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>exfiltration,</group>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100915" level="10">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <action>SetPassword</action>
    <user type="pcre2">^(?:admin|administrator|root)@</user>
    <description>[WARN] Password of a Zimbra administrator-class account reset by an administrator: $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- A zimlet is code that runs in every user's web client. -->
  <rule id="100916" level="8">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <action type="pcre2">^(?:CreateZimlet|DeleteZimlet|ModifyDelegatedAdminConstraints)$</action>
    <description>Zimbra zimlet or delegated-administrator constraint changed: $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1505</id>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <!-- An external LDAP bind password, an application password, a token or
       a two-factor secret in the changed attributes: Zimbra logs the value
       in clear, and the alert would copy it. Placed before 100918 (same
       level, loaded first). -->
  <rule id="100917" level="5">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <match type="pcre2">\bzimbra\w*(?:BindPassword|AuthPassword|CertificatePassword|SourcePassword|SpecificPassword|UCPassword|Secret|TokenKey|PreAuthKey)=[^;\s]</match>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <description>Zimbra administrative change setting a credential attribute (log line withheld, it contains the value): $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100918" level="5">
    <if_sid>100908</if_sid>
    <action type="pcre2">^(?:CreateAccount|DeleteAccount|RenameAccount|AddAccountAlias|RemoveAccountAlias|ChangePrimaryEmail[\w ]*|SetPassword|CreateDomain|DeleteDomain|ModifyDomain|ModifyConfig|CreateCos|CopyCos|ModifyCos|RenameCos|DeleteCos|CreateServer|ModifyServer|DeleteServer|DeleteMailbox|CreateGalSyncAccount|DeleteGalSyncAccount)$</action>
    <description>Zimbra account, domain or configuration change: $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
      <id>T1136</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

  <rule id="100919" level="12">
    <if_sid>100913,100917</if_sid>
    <srcip negate="yes">10.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">172.16.0.0/12</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">192.168.0.0/16</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">127.0.0.0/8</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">100.64.0.0/10</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fc00::/7</srcip>
    <srcip negate="yes">fe80::/10</srcip>
    <list field="srcip" lookup="not_address_match_key">etc/lists/zimbra-internal-networks</list>
    <options>no_full_log</options>
    <description>[HIGH] Zimbra credential or preauth key set from a public address (log line withheld): $(action) $(dstuser) by $(srcuser) from: $(srcip)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1098</id>
      <id>T1078</id>
    </mitre>
  </rule>

</group>
